      "turn_index": 0,
      "Question": "Perform a linear scan at 10 positions spaced +0.1 mm in X from the current position (relative +0.0 to +0.9 mm). Apply spike removal, 5th-order polynomial baseline correction, and vector-length normalization to each spectrum, and make a graph of the position-wise change of the 1001 cm-1 intensity.",
      "Ans": "The linear scan across 10 positions (X: 38.0 mm to 38.9 mm) has been completed and analyzed.\n\n### Measurement Summary\n- **Scan Range**: X = 38.0 mm $\\rightarrow$ 38.9 mm (Step: 0.1 mm).\n- **Y Position**: Fixed at 25.25 mm.\n- **Parameters**: Laser Power 40%, Exposure Time 0.2 s.\n\n### Data Processing Pipeline\nEach spectrum in the linear scan was processed using the following sequence:\n1. **Spike Removal**: A median filter was applied to identify and remove high-frequency impulsive noise (cosmic rays).\n2. **Baseline Correction**: A 5th-order polynomial fit was used to estimate and subtract the fluorescence background.\n3. **Normalization**: Vector-length normalization ($\\mathbf{y}_{norm} = \\frac{\\mathbf{y}}{\\|\\mathbf{y}\\|}$) was applied to ensure consistency across different positions.\n\n### Results & Analysis\nThe intensity of the spectral feature at **1001 cm⁻¹** was extracted via linear interpolation from the processed spectra and plotted against the X-coordinate.\n\n- **Intensity Trend**: The resulting plot shows that the normalized intensity at 1001 cm⁻¹ remains very low (fluctuating between approximately -0.06 and +0.03) across the entire scan range.\n- **Interpretation**: There is no significant Raman peak or systematic trend at 1001 cm⁻¹ in this region of the sample. The observed values are consistent with baseline noise following normalization.\n\n**Final Graph**: The analysis result image provides the position-wise intensity profile for the requested wavenumber.",
